/-! # Openness of the normal locus

Source: literature/stacks-project/algebra.tex, Algebra, Lemma Tag 0332
(blueprint: lem-weil-pbc-normal-locus-open)
-/

@[expose] public section

universe u
namespace AlgebraicGeometry

/-- The normal locus of a Noetherian domain with a normal principal localization is open.

Source: literature/stacks-project/algebra.tex, Algebra, Lemma Tag 0332
(blueprint: lem-weil-pbc-normal-locus-open)
Proof (following the nine steps of the node and Stacks Tag 0332):
1. The prime localizations of `R` and its localization away from the nonzero `f` are
   Noetherian domains. Use `lem-weil-pbc-localization-noetherian`; injectivity follows
   because all denominators are nonzero. Ring isomorphisms preserve normality, depth,
   dimension, and regularity.
2. The standard open `D(f)` lies in the normal locus `U`. At a prime not containing `f`,
   localization of `R_f` further at the image of the prime complement gives `R_p` by
   `lem-weil-pbc-localization-of-localization`. Normality is preserved by
   `lem-weil-pbc-localization-normal-domain`. Thus non-normal primes contain `f`.
3. Non-normality is stable under specialization: for `q` contained in `p`, `R_q` is a
   prime localization of `R_p`, by `lem-weil-pbc-primes-of-localization` and
   `lem-weil-pbc-localize-localization-at-prime`. Normality of `R_p` would imply normality
   of `R_q` by `lem-weil-pbc-localization-normal-domain`.
4. If `p` is outside `U`, apply `lem-weil-pbc-serre-criterion-normal` to the Noetherian
   local domain `R_p`. Failure of R1 or S2 gives a prime `q` contained in `p` such that
   `B = R_q` either has depth less than `min(2, dim B)`, or has dimension at most one
   and is not regular. Translate prime localizations using
   `lem-weil-pbc-primes-of-localization` and `lem-weil-pbc-localize-localization-at-prime`.
5. Serre's criterion shows that `B` is not normal. Step 2 gives `f` in `q`; its image in
   `B` is a nonzero element of the maximal ideal, hence a nonzerodivisor.
6. The depth of `B` is at most one. In the S2 case this follows from its depth bound.
   In the R1 case use `lem-weil-pbc-depth-le-dim-associated-prime` at the associated
   prime `(0)` of the domain `B` to bound depth by dimension.
7. By `lem-weil-pbc-depth-drops-by-one`, `B/fB` is nonzero of depth zero. Its maximal
   ideal is associated: otherwise `lem-weil-pbc-nonzerodivisor-if-maximal-not-associated`
   gives a nonzerodivisor in that ideal, a regular sequence of length one, contradicting
   depth zero (`def-weil-pbc-regular-sequence`, `def-weil-pbc-depth`).
8. Identify the localization of `R/fR` at `q` with `B/fB`. Then
   `lem-weil-pbc-noetherian-associated-prime-from-localization` implies that `q` is an
   associated prime of `R/fR`.
9. Let `E` consist of the associated primes of `R/fR` with non-normal localization.
   It is finite by `lem-weil-pbc-noetherian-associated-primes-finite`, since `R/fR` is
   generated by the class of one. Steps 4--8 place every prime outside `U` above a
   member of `E`, and step 3 gives the converse. Thus the complement of `U` is the
   finite union of the Zariski closed sets `V(q)` for `q` in `E`, so `U` is open.
-/
theorem normal_locus_isOpen
    {R : Type u} [CommRing R] [IsNoetherianRing R] [IsDomain R]
    (f : R) (hf : f ≠ 0)
    (hf_normal : IsDomain (Localization.Away f) ∧
      IsIntegrallyClosed (Localization.Away f)) :
    IsOpen {p : PrimeSpectrum R |
      IsDomain (Localization.AtPrime p.asIdeal) ∧
        IsIntegrallyClosed (Localization.AtPrime p.asIdeal)} := by
  sorry

end AlgebraicGeometry
